{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the codebase. Let me analyze the task:\n\n1. An orphan entry explains a Rekor attestation that the ledger doesn't hold\n2. The orphan has `body[\"entries_b64\"]` which is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes\n3. I need to add a function `_orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None` that:\n   - Decodes the entries\n   - Checks if they match the attested range (count, chain, head hash)\n   - Returns `None` if valid, otherwise a problem string containing \"range\", \"chain\", or \"head\"\n4. Wire it into wherever orphans are checked (likely in `cmd_monitor`)\n5. All existing tests must pass\n\nLet me look at how orphans are currently checked in `cmd_monitor`:\n\n```python\nexplained = {e[\"body\"][\"log_index\"]: e for e, _ in chain if e[\"kind\"] == \"orphan\"}\n...\nif idx not in explained:\n    unknown.append(row)\n    continue\no = explained[idx]\nbody = json.loads(base64.b64decode(v[\"body\"]))\nstmt = base64.b64decode(o[\"body\"][\"statement_b64\"])\nif body.get(\"kind\") != \"dsse\" or body[\"spec\"][\"payloadHash\"][\"value\"] != hashlib.sha256(stmt).hexdigest():\n    bad.append(f\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: its statement isn't the one Rekor holds for log index {idx}\")\n    continue\npred = (parse_json(stmt, f\"orphan entry {o['seq']} statement\") or {}).get(\"predicate\") or {}\nhead = pred.get(\"head_sha256\")\nif head in published and head not in current:\n    bad.append(f\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: the attested head {head} was published on {a.git_rev} and later removed\")\n    continue\norphans.append(dict(row, explained_by_entry=o[\"seq\"], attested_range=f\"{pred.get('from_seq')}-{pred.get('to_seq')}\"))\n```\n\nI need to add a check here that validates the orphan's `entries_b64` against the predicate. Let me implement the function and wire it in.\n\nFirst, let me check if orphan entries already have an `entries_b64` field. Looking at the `check_body` function for orphans:\n\n```python\nelse:  # orphan: explains a Rekor attestation by the writer that the ledger doesn't hold\n    allowed = {\"by\", \"log_index\", \"reason\", \"statement_b64\"}\n    if _need(body, \"log_index\", int, where) < 0 or not _need(body, \"reason\", str, where).strip():\n        raise Bad(f\"{where}: orphan needs log_index >= 0 and a reason\")\n    try:  # the orphaned attestation's exact signed statement; monitor checks it against Rekor's payload hash\n        base64.b64decode(_need(body, \"statement_b64\", str, where), validate=True)\n    except ValueError:\n        raise Bad(f\"{where}: statement_b64 must be base64\")\n```\n\nSo currently orphans only have `by`, `log_index`, `reason`, `statement_b64`. The task says `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes. So I need to:\n1. Update `check_body` to allow and validate `entries_b64` for orphans\n2. Add the `_orphan_entries_problem` function\n3. Wire it into `cmd_monitor`\n\nWait, the task says \"add a function to record/record.py: _orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None\". It also says \"Wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\"\n\nLet me re-read the orphan schema. The task says:\n- `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes (as produced by `R.canonical(entry)`).\n\nBut currently the allowed fields for orphan are only `by`, `log_index`, `reason`, `statement_b64`. So I need to add `entries_b64` to the allowed fields and validate it.\n\nLet me implement this step by step.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now I understand the codebase. Let me analyze the task:\\n\\n1. An orphan entry explains a Rekor attestation that the ledger doesn't hold\\n2. The orphan has `body[\\\"entries_b64\\\"]` which is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes\\n3. I need to add a function `_orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None` that:\\n   - Decodes the entries\\n   - Checks if they match the attested range (count, chain, head hash)\\n   - Returns `None` if valid, otherwise a problem string containing \\\"range\\\", \\\"chain\\\", or \\\"head\\\"\\n4. Wire it into wherever orphans are checked (likely in `cmd_monitor`)\\n5. All existing tests must pass\\n\\nLet me look at how orphans are currently checked in `cmd_monitor`:\\n\\n```python\\nexplained = {e[\\\"body\\\"][\\\"log_index\\\"]: e for e, _ in chain if e[\\\"kind\\\"] == \\\"orphan\\\"}\\n...\\nif idx not in explained:\\n    unknown.append(row)\\n    continue\\no = explained[idx]\\nbody = json.loads(base64.b64decode(v[\\\"body\\\"]))\\nstmt = base64.b64decode(o[\\\"body\\\"][\\\"statement_b64\\\"])\\nif body.get(\\\"kind\\\") != \\\"dsse\\\" or body[\\\"spec\\\"][\\\"payloadHash\\\"][\\\"value\\\"] != hashlib.sha256(stmt).hexdigest():\\n    bad.append(f\\\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: its statement isn't the one Rekor holds for log index {idx}\\\")\\n    continue\\npred = (parse_json(stmt, f\\\"orphan entry {o['seq']} statement\\\") or {}).get(\\\"predicate\\\") or {}\\nhead = pred.get(\\\"head_sha256\\\")\\nif head in published and head not in current:\\n    bad.append(f\\\"orphan entry {o['seq']}: the attested head {head} was published on {a.git_rev} and later removed\\\")\\n    continue\\norphans.append(dict(row, explained_by_entry=o[\\\"seq\\\"], attested_range=f\\\"{pred.get('from_seq')}-{pred.get('to_seq')}\\\"))\\n```\\n\\nI need to add a check here that validates the orphan's `entries_b64` against the predicate. Let me implement the function and wire it in.\\n\\nFirst, let me check if orphan entries already have an `entries_b64` field. Looking at the `check_body` function for orphans:\\n\\n```python\\nelse:  # orphan: explains a Rekor attestation by the writer that the ledger doesn't hold\\n    allowed = {\\\"by\\\", \\\"log_index\\\", \\\"reason\\\", \\\"statement_b64\\\"}\\n    if _need(body, \\\"log_index\\\", int, where) < 0 or not _need(body, \\\"reason\\\", str, where).strip():\\n        raise Bad(f\\\"{where}: orphan needs log_index >= 0 and a reason\\\")\\n    try:  # the orphaned attestation's exact signed statement; monitor checks it against Rekor's payload hash\\n        base64.b64decode(_need(body, \\\"statement_b64\\\", str, where), validate=True)\\n    except ValueError:\\n        raise Bad(f\\\"{where}: statement_b64 must be base64\\\")\\n```\\n\\nSo currently orphans only have `by`, `log_index`, `reason`, `statement_b64`. The task says `orphan[\\\"body\\\"][\\\"entries_b64\\\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes. So I need to:\\n1. Update `check_body` to allow and validate `entries_b64` for orphans\\n2. Add the `_orphan_entries_problem` function\\n3. Wire it into `cmd_monitor`\\n\\nWait, the task says \\\"add a function to record/record.py: _orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None\\\". It also says \\\"Wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\\\"\\n\\nLet me re-read the orphan schema. The task says:\\n- `orphan[\\\"body\\\"][\\\"entries_b64\\\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes (as produced by `R.canonical(entry)`).\\n\\nBut currently the allowed fields for orphan are only `by`, `log_index`, `reason`, `statement_b64`. So I need to add `entries_b64` to the allowed fields and validate it.\\n\\nLet me implement this step by step.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me understand the task better by looking at how orphans are handled in the monitor command and what the orphan schema currently allows.\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-c47f9990-46d9-49c6-b9c2-b4dfd8bc653d","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workspace/record && python3 -m unittest test_record -v 2>&1 | head -100"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"nvidia/nemotron-3-ul