{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Hmm, the orphan with wrong `entries_b64` was accepted during ingest. That's because the ingest command only validates the schema of the orphan entry, not the content of `entries_b64` against the statement. The validation of `entries_b64` against the predicate happens in `cmd_monitor`. This is by design - the monitor is what checks that orphans correctly explain attestations.\n\nBut wait, should we also validate during ingest? The task says \"Wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\" The monitor is where orphans are checked against Rekor attestations. The ingest just validates the schema.\n\nLet me check if there's any other place where orphans are checked. The monitor is the main place. The check-anchors command doesn't check orphans.\n\nActually, looking at the task again: \"Wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\" The monitor is the place where orphans are checked against Rekor. So my implementation is correct.\n\nBut wait - the test I just did shows that an orphan with wrong entries_b64 is accepted during ingest. This is expected because:\n1. The statement_b64 is valid base64\n2. The entries_b64 is a valid list of base64 strings\n3. The schema validation passes\n\nThe actual validation against the predicate happens in monitor when it compares against the Rekor attestation.\n\nThis seems correct. The orphan entry just stores the statement and the hidden entries; the monitor validates that they match what Rekor has.\n\nLet me run the full test suite again to make sure everything still passes.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Hmm, the orphan with wrong `entries_b64` was accepted during ingest. That's because the ingest command only validates the schema of the orphan entry, not the content of `entries_b64` against the statement. The 